# Brent root finding

Brent's method solves a scalar equation `f(x) = 0` by combining interpolation
with bisection. It uses only function values and retains a bracket throughout
the search.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/brent/struct.BrentRoot.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                               |
  | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Equation    | A continuous, deterministic scalar function                |
  | Evaluations | A fallible function-value callback                        |
  | Search      | One root inside a supplied bracket                        |
  | Interval    | Finite, strictly ordered endpoints with a sign change      |
  | Parameters  | Scalar `f32` or `f64`, without optional features            |

## When to use it

Consider Brent as a general starting point when you can bracket a scalar root
and do not have derivatives. Interpolation can make it substantially faster
than [bisection](../bisection/), while fallback steps preserve progress when
the interpolation model is poor. Compare [TOMS 748](../toms748/) when function
evaluations are expensive and the function is smooth.

If reliable derivatives are cheap, compare [Newton](../newton/) or
[Halley](../halley/). All of these Basin methods require a sign-changing
interval, or an exact endpoint root. An even-multiplicity root generally has
no sign change, and an interval with several roots does not select a
particular one.

## How it works

[Brent (1971)](#ref-brent-1971) combines secant and inverse quadratic
interpolation with interval halving. Basin keeps a sign-changing pair and
recent evaluated points. Two distinct function values support a secant
proposal; additional history permits inverse quadratic interpolation.

The solver accepts interpolation only when its size and recent progress pass
the safeguards. Otherwise, it bisects the current bracket. Each trial
evaluates the function once and updates the interval. This combines fast
local interpolation with the reliability of bracket reduction, provided the
function satisfies the continuity contract.

## Example

This example first discovers a bracket for the positive zero of `x² - 2`.
The initial interval `[0, 1]` does not bracket the root, so `RootBracketer`
expands it while respecting the lower domain limit. Always check the bracket
search result before starting the solve.

<!-- compile: root-brent -->
```rust
use basin::{BrentRoot, RootBracketer};
use std::convert::Infallible;

fn main() {
    let function = |x| Ok::<_, Infallible>(x * x - 2.0);
    let search = RootBracketer::new(0.0, 1.0)
        .with_lower_bound(0.0)
        .bracket(function)
        .unwrap();
    assert!(search.bracketed());

    let (lower, upper) = search.bracket();
    let result = BrentRoot::new(lower, upper)
        .with_absolute_position_tolerance(1e-10)
        .with_max_iter(100)
        .solve(function)
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.converged());
    assert!((result.root() - 2.0_f64.sqrt()).abs() < 1e-10);
    assert_eq!(result.value(), result.root() * result.root() - 2.0);
    let total_evaluations = search.function_evals() + result.function_evals();
    assert!(total_evaluations > result.function_evals());
}
```

If you already know a bracket, pass its endpoints directly to `BrentRoot::new`.
The interval belongs to the root finder; it needs no `BoxBounds` declaration,
optimization state, or `Executor`.

## Practical considerations

### Check continuity and domain limits

The endpoints must be finite and strictly ordered, their difference must be
finite, and their values must have opposite signs unless an endpoint is
exactly a root. All evaluated function values must be finite. A sign change
across a pole or jump can shrink the bracket without locating a zero.

An automatic bracket search can skip roots of a nonmonotonic function.
Use domain knowledge to choose seeds and optional search limits. Failure to
find a bracket does not prove there is no root.

### Set position accuracy and an iteration limit

`with_absolute_position_tolerance` defaults to `1e-12`, and
`with_relative_position_tolerance` defaults to `4ε` for the scalar type.
Convergence requires an exact zero or a bracket width at most
`absolute + relative * abs(root)`. These settings bound position error under
the function contract; inspect `value()` separately if residual size matters.

The absolute tolerance must be finite and positive, and the relative tolerance
must be finite and at least `4ε`. Invalid tolerance settings panic. For `f32`,
choose accuracy consistent with its larger machine epsilon.

The default limit is 100 evaluated trial steps. `with_max_iter(0)` still
checks the endpoint values, stopping early on an exact lower-endpoint root.
Reaching the limit returns a clean `RootResult` with reason `MaxIter`.
Check `converged()` or `reason()` before treating the estimate as a solution.

### Handle typed errors

Brent returns `BrentRootError<E, F>`, where `E` is the callback's error type.
Its variants distinguish an invalid interval, a missing sign change, a
non-finite function value, and an application error preserved in `Evaluation`.
The other root solvers use `RootError<E, F>`.

## Implementation

`RootResult` retains the root estimate, its signed function value, the final
ordered bracket, the termination reason, and work counts. Each `solve` starts
fresh. Bracket discovery and solving reevaluate their own endpoints, so add
both stages' `function_evals()` when measuring the complete search.

For Brent, `callback_evals()` equals `function_evals()`, including endpoint
evaluations; derivative counts are zero. Scalar `f32` and `f64` require no
linear-algebra backend or optional feature and work on WASM.

